<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Equal Rights Amendment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ERA, Lucretia Mott Amendment</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Equal Rights Amendment is a proposed amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> that would explicitly prohibit sex discrimination. Written by Alice Paul and Crystal Eastman, it was first introduced in Congress in December 1923 and, with the rise of the women's movement, passed the House of Representatives in 1971 and the Senate in 1972. Its ratification deadline of 1979, later extended to 1982, passed with the amendment three states short of the 38 needed, after opposition led by Phyllis Schlafly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> ratified it between 2017 and 2020, but its status is still debated and it is not part of the Constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/equal-rights-amendment.jpg" alt="Equal Rights Amendment" width="330" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GeorgeLouis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_holding_Equal_Rights_Amendment_sign_in_Los_Angeles,_California,_with_two_men,_one_of_them_yawning.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 25 September 1921 the National Woman's Party announced a campaign for a constitutional amendment guaranteeing women equal rights with men. Its first text forbade any political, civil or legal disabilities or inequalities on account of sex or of marriage. Alice Paul, the party's head, believed that the Nineteenth Amendment would not be enough to ensure equal treatment, and in 1923 at Seneca Falls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, she revised the wording. In its early decades middle-class women largely supported it, while those speaking for the working class often opposed it, arguing that employed women needed special protections regarding working conditions and hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text approved by Congress has two operative sections. The first provides that equality of rights under the law shall not be denied or abridged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> or by any State on account of sex; the second gives Congress power to enforce it by appropriate legislation. The resolving clause required ratification by three-fourths of the states within seven years. Supporters held that it would end legal distinctions between men and women in divorce, property and employment. Opponents argued that it would remove protections from women, expose them to conscription and combat roles, and threaten alimony and housewives' Social Security benefits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After pressure from the women's movement, including NOW's the disruption of a Senate hearing in February 1970 and the Women's Strike for Equality that August, Representative Martha Griffiths reintroduced the amendment in 1971. It went to the states on 22 March 1972. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> ratified it that same day, 22 states ratified it during 1972 and eight more early in 1973, but only five between 1974 and 1977. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, supporters of Phyllis Schlafly lobbied legislators with homemade bread, jams and apple pies. South Dakota voted in 1979 that its own ratification would lapse at the original deadline.</p><p>The amendment has been reintroduced in every session of Congress since 1982; in 1983 it fell six votes short of two-thirds on the House floor, its last floor vote. From the mid-1990s supporters pursued a three-state strategy, citing the Twenty-seventh Amendment, ratified in 1992 after pending since 1789. Lawsuits followed on both sides from 2019. On 17 January 2025, three days before leaving office, President Joe Biden declared the amendment the law of the land, but the declaration had no formal effect, and the National Archives said it did not intend to certify it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Equal Rights Amendment” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The National American Woman Suffrage Association became the League of Women Voters in 1920, Alice Paul’s National Woman’s Party proposed an <b>Equal Rights Amendment</b> in 1923, and the International Woman Suffrage Alliance became the International Alliance of Women.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the National Organization for Women, founded in 1966, campaigned for the <b>Equal Rights Amendment</b>, which did not pass, and Roe v. Wade enunciated a woman’s right to choose.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
